            ```
            <div class="col-lg-8" align="justify">
                <h3><b>Common Customer Segmentation Challenges and How to Address Them</b></h3>

                <p>
                    Organizations may face challenges when customer information is incomplete, customer
                    groups overlap, or segmentation criteria do not provide meaningful business value.
                    A structured approach can help organizations create more useful and manageable segments.
                </p>

                <ul class="list-unstyled">
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Incomplete Customer Data: Improve data collection and management practices to create a more complete understanding of customer characteristics and behavior.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Overlapping Segments: Review segmentation criteria and establish clear definitions so customer groups remain meaningful and manageable.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Too Many Customer Groups: Focus on segments that provide meaningful differences and can be connected to practical business actions.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Outdated Segments: Regularly review customer behavior and update segments when customer requirements or market conditions change.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Difficulty Using Segment Insights: Connect segmentation results with product planning, marketing strategies, sales activities, and customer experience initiatives.</p>
                    </li>
                </ul><br />

                <h3><b>Types of Customer Segmentation</b></h3>

                <p>
                    Organizations can use different segmentation approaches depending on their business
                    objectives and available customer information. Combining relevant approaches can
                    provide a more complete view of customer groups.
                </p>

                <ul class="list-unstyled">
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Demographic Segmentation: Group customers using relevant characteristics such as age range, occupation, organization size, or other applicable attributes.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Geographic Segmentation: Organize customers according to location, region, market, or other geographic factors that influence customer requirements.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Behavioral Segmentation: Group customers according to product usage, interactions, purchasing behavior, engagement, or other observable activities.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Needs-Based Segmentation: Group customers according to their specific requirements, problems, expectations, and desired outcomes.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Value-Based Segmentation: Identify customer groups based on business value, relationship potential, engagement, or other relevant measures.</p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
    ```

</section>

